  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "Andrew divided some apples into six equal piles. Boris divided the same number of apples into five equal piles. Boris noticed that each of his piles contained two more apples than each of Andrew's piles. How many apples did Andrew have?",
  "image": null,
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "30"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "55"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "60"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "75"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "90"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "C",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Use an equation",
    "body": "Let Andrew's piles have $a$ apples, so there are $6a$ apples. Boris's piles have $a+2$ apples, so there are $5(a+2)$ apples. Then $6a=5a+10$, so $a=10$ and the total is $6\\times10=60$.\n\n**Answer: (C) 60**"
   },
   {
    "title": "Use multiples of 30",
    "body": "The number of apples is a multiple of 6 and of 5, so it is $30k$. Andrew's piles hold $5k$ apples and Boris's hold $6k$, which is $k$ more. This difference is 2, so $k=2$ and the number of apples is $60$.\n\n**Answer: (C) 60**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "algebra",
   "arithmetic"
  ],
  "difficulty": 2,
